There is a much larger family

Factorization and partition are two members of a much broader family of “structural transforms.”

Something like:

Operator	Question
Factorization	What irreducible pieces produced this?
Partition	How can this whole be divided?
Composition	How do pieces become a whole?
Aggregation	What larger unit emerges?
Compression	What can be discarded while preserving function?
Expansion	What hidden detail can be made explicit?
Projection	What does this look like from one perspective?
Embedding	How does this fit inside a larger space?
Quotienting	Which differences can be ignored?
Refinement	How can distinctions be increased?
Coarsening	Which distinctions should collapse?
Clustering	Which elements naturally belong together?
Ordering	What sequence best explains this?
Hierarchy	What contains what?
Networking	What relates to what?
Symmetrization	What remains invariant under transformation?
Dualization	What complementary description exists?
Translation	How is the same invariant expressed elsewhere?

Notice the pattern.

These aren’t mathematical operations alone.

They’re ways of changing representation while preserving (or intentionally changing) certain invariants.

That seems very aligned with your broader interest in translation across domains.

